  "body": "## What\n\n`publisher.render` neutralised exactly one handle \u2014 the agent's own \u2014 and wrote everything else through verbatim. That text is model output over an attacker-influenced tree, posted by an account with a real token.\n\n## Why it matters\n\n| In a finding | What GitHub did with it |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| `@someone`, `@org/team` | notified that account or team **from the agent's account** \u2014 a harassment / spam vector |\n| `#123`, `owner/repo#123`, `GH-123` | cross-referenced an unrelated issue, from the agent |\n| `<sub>`, `<details>`, `<!-- -->` | rendered \u2014 a forged second \"Automated review\u2026\" trailer, or text hidden from a reader |\n| >65 536 chars | a `422` **after** the tokens were spent, re-offered on every claim forever |\n| a leaked token | posted as-is |\n\nRoadmap item **A6**.\n\n## How\n\nNew `src/pr_review_agent/sanitise.py`, pure functions over strings:\n\n- one alternation regex, **one pass over the original** \u2014 escaping `@` as `&#64;` introduces a `#` that a second pass would escape again;\n- **prose only**, sharing `strip_non_prose` with `triggers.mention` so the two cannot disagree about where prose is. GitHub does not mention, cross-reference or render HTML inside a code span or fence either, and an entity there would show the reader `&#64;` in what is meant to be a code sample;\n- **escaped, not stripped** \u2014 a reader still sees `@someone` and `#123`, because \"see #123\" is saying something worth reading. What it stops being is a link the agent pulled.\n\n`render` gained `_fit`: cap at `MAX_BODY_CHARS = 60 000`, dropping **whole sections lowest-severity first** (`SECTIONS` is already in the order a reader needs them), with a truncation note so a partial review never looks complete. Only when the highest-severity section alone is oversized is prose cut mid-sentence.\n\n`Publisher.secrets` (the live `GITHUB_TOKEN`, passed by `daemon.run`) is checked against the body before posting. A hit is `PublishOutcome.REFUSED`: logged at ERROR *without* the body, and stamped so it is not re-offered \u2014 re-running would spend again to render the same comment.\n\n**Deliberately not escaped: bare commit SHAs.** They auto-link to a commit in the repository the review is posted on \u2014 a link, not a notification and not a cross-reference somewhere else. Escaping them would mangle legitimate review prose for no gain.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_sanitise.py` (23) plus 3 in `tests/test_publisher.py`: one per escaping rule; the three prose exemptions; escaping happens once; the property that no word-start `@` survives the prose of a rendered review; nits go through the same rule; the trailer is the only HTML left; the cap drops Nits before Blocking; an oversized single section is cut but still marked; and the canary refuses, is not re-offered, and does not fire when no secrets are configured.\n\n## Gate\n\n```\n1047 passed, 2 skipped, 1 deselected, 1 warning in 73.91s\nruff format / ruff check: clean\npylint src 9.95/10   pylint tests 9.36/10\npyright: 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 informations\n```\n\nReleases **1.1.3**. Based on `fix/62-config-template-placeholders` (#94).\n\nFixes #64.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
